hat these  these dna will be read that the dna will  be read  and examples of factors that induce  epigenetic changes  are listed here social interactions  they're all here  nutrition smoking and so  that dna how it's read how much it's  going to be expressed or not  will depend on your experience so this  is what i mean by the  genes are listening to the environment  and just in a very um  pictorial way we see that when  the uh early environment is adverse  this dna that wraps around other  proteins  in the chromatin it's almost like it's  covered up and it can't be read or  expressed  and in these more positive environments  the dna is easier to read  and in epigenetics it's not turning on  or off this gene expression it's more  like a dimmer switch that  up or down regulates the capacity for  that gene to express  itself and the work of many people in  the room and others has shown that early  adversity changes the likelihood  that some genes will be read just like  bruce said about the glucocorticoid  receptor gene  involved in stress and that how we cope  with stress how our brain develops and  works  and how we fight disease okay so one  quick example from michael meaney and  moishe ziff  we have a rant mom and that mommy licks  her pups  a lot or a little and so this is how it  would work if this is epigenetic  we have the mom who licks a little bit  and the one who  licks a lot and these are her pups who  get licked a little bit or a lot  but you can cross foster them and then  ask the question what happens with these  guys  when they have a biological mum that  licks a lot but their experience is to  be licked a little bit  do they end up being mothers that have  to do with  their biological background or their  experience or some combination of both  and what they've shown is that it's the  foster mum that matters  for so for this glucocorticoid receptor  you can look at  how much of the gene is expressed what  matters is a foster mum so here we have  the 